#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060690} :activity/id #uuid "95e1ac2b-a3ab-570a-aa6c-322637d3df72" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050882 :topic/id #uuid "6e1bba25-5964-5d7f-844a-632e8e502d16" :topic/math-academy-id 248} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060690 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060987 :tutorial/content "Of particular importance in mathematical proofs are so-called **biconditional statements.** These are statements of the form\n\n$$\n(P\\Rightarrow Q) \\land (Q\\Rightarrow P).\n$$\n\nIn words, a biconditional statement states that *\"$P$ implies $Q,$ **and** $Q$ implies $P.$\"* \n\nLet's construct a truth table for this biconditional statement.\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,Q\\,$ | $\\,P\\Rightarrow Q\\,$ | $\\,Q\\Rightarrow P\\,$ | $\\,(P \\Rightarrow Q)\\land (Q \\Rightarrow P)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n\nBiconditional statements are so important that we give them a special symbol.\n\n$$\nP\\Leftrightarrow Q\n$$\n\nSo, we now have the following logical equivalence:\n\n$$\nP \\Leftrightarrow Q \\equiv (P \\Rightarrow Q) \\land (Q \\Rightarrow P)\n$$\n\nThe truth table for $P\\Leftrightarrow Q$ is as follows:\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,Q\\,$ | $\\,P\\Leftrightarrow Q\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n\nAccording to the truth table, $P\\Leftrightarrow Q$ is true if $P$ and $Q$ have the *same* truth value. Otherwise, it is false. We can express this as another logical equivalence:\n\n$$\nP\\Leftrightarrow Q \\equiv \\big(P \\land Q\\big) \\lor \\big(\\neg P \\land \\neg Q\\big)\n$$\n\nThere are many ways to say $P\\Leftrightarrow Q$ in words. Among the most common are the following:\n\n- *$P$ is equivalent to $Q$*\n\n- *$P$ if and only if $Q$*\n\n- *If $P$ then $Q,$ and if $Q$ then $P$*\n\n- *$P$ implies $Q$, and $Q$ implies $P$*" :tutorial/id #uuid "093aafe9-8f7e-5894-aa0f-80d9837de231" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7981 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d64cb2fb-f12e-53f4-854b-20e14ebe1763" :step/math-academy-id 180138 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060691}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060691 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059777 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059418 :question/id #uuid "15d6b6c0-af70-5d22-bfdc-b2c0aaf98de5" :question/math-academy-id "e-12717"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe8eb0bd-6d37-5eb8-98fd-796b2af992f7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049269}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379322} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412690}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Equivalence Truth Table"} :step/id #uuid "16368019-6e3a-59cb-aff9-396cc5a523ae" :step/math-academy-id 180139 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060692}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060692 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059778 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059417 :question/id #uuid "b5abe855-4626-5575-a03e-04a377eef82b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12716"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35e42262-e78c-5553-acfe-739684165622" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049805}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060148} {:db/id #edb/ref